Claude responded: That error is coming from the cerr statement in loadData():
That error is coming from the cerr statement in loadData():

cpp
cerr << "Error: " << strerror(errno) << endl;
This means the file failed to open, which is likely because the data file cannot be found at the path provided. A few common culprits:

The file isn't in the same directory as the executable — the program looks for the file relative to where it's being run from, not where the source code is
The filename is misspelled — e.g. "graph1.txt" vs "Graph1.txt" (case sensitive on Linux/Mac)
The working directory is wrong — if running from an IDE, the working directory may not be where you expect it to be
The file extension is hidden — on Windows, the file might actually be named graph1.txt.txt if file extensions are hidden in Explorer
The quickest fix is to either:

Place the data file in the same directory as the executable
Or provide the full absolute path to the file (e.g. "/home/user/project/graph1.txt")
Which environment are you running this in?

